        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Where should a cup holder go on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              To the side and slightly forward, on a side handrail ahead of where your hand rests or on an upright beside the console, with the cup outboard of the frame. Avoid positions directly above the console or the motor hood, where a spill can reach the electronics, and keep the safety key and stop button clear.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why are treadmill cup holders so small?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Built-in holders are moulded into the console and sized for a standard sports bottle, so large tumblers and wide bottles do not fit. An add-on clamp holder, adjustable bottle cage or strap-on pouch on a handrail or upright takes larger drinks and keeps them off the console.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Will a bike bottle holder fit a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Often, if the rail is round and within the clamp's range. Measure the rail's diameter and shape first. Bike cages hold bottles well but rarely suit cups with handles. Thick or oval uprights need a strap-on holder instead of a clamp.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I use a treadmill tray while running?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is not a good idea. Trays sit over the centre of the machine, and at running pace the frame vibrates enough to rattle drinks and devices. Most tray makers describe them for walking. For running, use a rigid holder on a side rail and a bottle with a sealed lid.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What should I do if I spill a drink on my treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Stop the belt and pull the safety key. If liquid has reached the console, motor hood or anywhere near electrical parts, switch off and unplug the treadmill before cleaning, wipe and dry everything, and do not restart it until it is completely dry. If it shows faults afterwards, contact the maker or a technician.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do treadmill trays fit every treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. Trays strap across both handrails and list a range of handrail spacings, and many do not suit sloping or tilted rails. Measure between the outside edges of your rails at the height the tray will sit, and check that it will not cover the display, safety key or stop button.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

